/Users/mac/Desktop/Terminal Bench/Terminal_2.0_Harbor_Harness/Input_Bundle_QC/05_Alignment_Integrity_QC.md:55:- AL-14 Reward-file tampering: instruction never mentions reward paths (Gate 01); test.sh clears stale reward files, locks `/logs/verifier`, writes no reward itself and ends with `exec rewardkit` (Gate 03 TS-6); no check can raise on agent output and leave the run without a reward (TS-29); no agent subprocess outlives its check to rewrite `reward.json` (TS-24); and `tests/reward.toml` still matches both channels' positive totals (TS-10). Re-confirm the combination end to end.
/Users/mac/Desktop/Terminal Bench/Terminal_2.0_Harbor_Harness/Input_Bundle_QC/05_Alignment_Integrity_QC.md:56:- AL-15 Offline contract consistency: every layer must agree that the task runs with no network. `[environment] network_mode` is pinned to `"no-network"` for every task, so there is no per-task policy to look up - the comparison is between the layers and that fixed fact, not between the layers and a value. Nothing may assume network at solve time or grade time: an instruction that tells the agent to download, a Dockerfile that fetches at run time rather than build time, a solution that installs what it needs, or a test that reaches an endpoint are each a fail. The `[verifier]` and `[agent]` network lines are NOT part of this comparison: the judge needs egress to score the judged channel and the agent harness needs egress to reach its own model, neither of which is the task being online. An offline task with an online verifier and an online agent harness is the normal combination rather than a contradiction. Do not raise a finding merely because the bundle never mentions the network; silence is the expected state. If the work genuinely requires an outside endpoint, say so here as a design finding against the offline contract.
/Users/mac/Desktop/Terminal Bench/Terminal_2.0_Harbor_Harness/Input_Bundle_QC/05_Alignment_Integrity_QC.md:65:- AL-19 No credential or secret exfiltration: no credential shipped anywhere (including a literal value in `[verifier.env]`). Apart from the pinned `OPENAI_API_KEY` placeholder, nothing reads environment variables for credentials, ~/.ssh, ~/.aws, or any credential store, and nothing transmits or stages such values. Fail.
/Users/mac/Desktop/Terminal Bench/Terminal_2.0_Harbor_Harness/Input_Bundle_QC/05_Alignment_Integrity_QC.md:66:- AL-20 No network exfiltration or supply-chain risk: no curl/wget piped to a shell, no downloading and executing code from URLs, nothing that transmits bundle contents or grading data outward. The task runs offline, so there must be no outbound calls at all in the instruction, the Dockerfile's runtime path, the solution or the tests; AL-15 owns the plain "this cannot work offline" case, while what stays a fail here, and would stay one under any policy, is malicious use: exfiltration of secrets, bundle contents or grading data, and downloading then executing code. Fail. One exception: the official uv installer piped to a shell at build time in `environment/Dockerfile`, exactly `curl -LsSf https://astral.sh/uv/install.sh | sh`, as in the recommended DS-29 block. Any other piped download, or this one anywhere else (tests/Dockerfile, solve.sh, test.sh, the checks), still fails.
