/Users/mac/Desktop/Terminal Bench/Terminal_2.0_Harbor_Harness/Input_Bundle_QC/Final_checklist.md:305:- FC-S19 [verifier.env] carries exactly one key, OPENAI_API_KEY, holding exactly the `${OPENAI_API_KEY:-}` placeholder the template shows. The block is the same in every task and is copied, not authored, so any deviation is either a typo that stops the judge from running or, in the case of a value that is not a placeholder at all, a real API key pasted into a file that ships to the client. Read that second case as a credential leak (gate 05 AL-19), not as a formatting problem.
/Users/mac/Desktop/Terminal Bench/Terminal_2.0_Harbor_Harness/Input_Bundle_QC/Final_checklist.md:382:That is the host, not the bundle. Harbor checks whether the Docker daemon's kernel can enforce the policy (it probes for `CONFIG_NFT_FIB_INET`); Docker Desktop's VM kernel does not have it, so Harbor declines any policy it cannot enforce, and `allowlist` is rejected for exactly the same reason `no-network` is. `[agent]` must move with the others: Harbor checks the agent-phase policy before the run starts, whatever the agent is, so an `[agent]` allowlist over a public `[environment]` still refuses to start, even for oracle and nop. Opening the network changes no score: the oracle does the work offline from the shipped data, nop does nothing, the stub only touches the declared paths, and the copy has no judge to reach. Keep the two claims separate in the sign-off: the scores are evidence about grading, not evidence that the declared offline policy is enforceable. The policy itself stays a read-only check (FC-S12, IN-11, DS-11, AL-15).
/Users/mac/Desktop/Terminal Bench/Terminal_2.0_Harbor_Harness/Input_Bundle_QC/Final_checklist.md:491:- [ ] CL-4 Partial credit tracks partial correctness. Count the independently discoverable facts the deliverable carries (per field, per setting, per row), then read how the tests compare them. One wrong fact should cost about one fact's worth of reward. What fails is an answer that is almost entirely right scoring as though it were entirely wrong, and that happens two ways, both worth looking for: one whole-object equality settling N independent facts at once, and a pile of separately weighted tests that all key off that same single comparison. Report what an answer with exactly one fact wrong would score. Two things are not this defect. An instruction that states out loud that only an exact match counts has set that bar deliberately. And weight distribution by itself is fine: one test legitimately owning most of the reward because it checks the main outcome is good design, not a cliff, so do not go looking for a percentage. What matters is whether the curve can tell a near miss from a total miss. The client measured the failure: six of seven hidden settings correct scored 0.041, the same as none of them, because 14 tests worth +5 each all keyed off one dict comparison - 70 of 73 points turning on a single word.
/Users/mac/Desktop/Terminal Bench/Terminal_2.0_Harbor_Harness/Input_Bundle_QC/04_Judge_QC.md:92:- JD-15 What a criterion may require, and where that requirement comes from. A bundle grades two independent things. Whether the work came out right, and whether the agent stayed inside the rules it was given. Neither excuses the other. An agent can produce a flawless artifact while ignoring every rule it was handed, and that is a real failure, because in a real deployment the rule is often the part that matters most: leave production config alone, use the approved client, stay inside the files you were assigned. Criteria therefore come in three kinds and the rules differ for each, so classify every criterion before you judge it. Nothing in the file declares which of the three it is, so the classification is entirely yours and you make it from the description. The one claim the annotations do make is `type`, and a constraint criterion carries `type = "instruction following"` (JD-16), so read that value as a claim to check rather than an answer to accept. The two mismatches both matter. A criterion you read as an unstated standard but labelled `type = "instruction following"` escapes the conduct test, and a criterion enforcing a stated rule but labelled with any other category escapes JD-16's demand for a quoted sentence. Where your reading and the label disagree, judge the criterion by your reading and report the label as the defect. An OUTCOME criterion asserts something about what was produced: the artifact's content, its format, a value inside it, a fact in the final answer. That is a specification item, and an agent can only meet it if it could have known. Two grounds count and either one is enough: the instruction states it, or it is determinate in the input the agent is handed, meaning any directory `environment/Dockerfile` copies into the agent image. The prompt need not point at the file, because working out the shipped data is the task (gate 01 IN-8). Name the sentence it traces to, or the shipped fact it reads, and the file in either case (gate 05 re-checks the same thing across both layers). An outcome criterion resting on something in neither place is an oracle expectation the honest agent could not reach: fail. Most outcome criteria also restate a test, so run JD-8 on them as well. A CONSTRAINT criterion enforces a rule of engagement that the contract states out loud: how the output must be shaped, which files may be touched, which tool must be used, what order the work must happen in, what must be left alone. A rule of engagement is not a fact about the data, so the loose grounding above does not reach it: it has to be STATED, in a sentence the agent can read, in the instruction or in any file inside the input directories `environment/Dockerfile` copies into the agent image, because a rule the agent can read is a rule it was given, while a prohibition cannot be inferred from a CSV (gate 01 IN-8). Rules about the shape of the output are the exception and keep the outcome burden, a shipped example included, since a shape is a fact about the artifact rather than a restriction on the agent (JD-16). And a rule that cannot be stated without disclosing the approach is not a constraint either: demanding its sentence would force the method into the prompt, which gate 01 fails under IN-14, so judge it in the conduct branch below. It is graded because it was stated, and compliance is the requirement in itself. It does NOT have to make a correct end state more likely, and you must not ask it to. A rule that narrows the agent's freedom without improving its answer is exactly what this class is for. JD-16 says what makes one valid; JD-17 keeps constraints from becoming the whole task. A CONDUCT criterion is a standard of work the author holds the agent to without writing it anywhere: derivation method, verification habits, honest reporting, restraint, safe behaviour. Nothing told the agent it would be judged on this, so the criterion has to earn its place on fairness grounds instead. That is what the two steps below are for. They are a substitute for a missing sentence, which is why they apply here and never to a constraint criterion, where the sentence is sitting in the instruction. Decide each conduct criterion in two steps. Step 1: would every competent route to a correct end state satisfy this criterion? If yes it is legitimate and needs no instruction sentence, because it constrains nothing. Verifying your own work, grounding the solution in the shipped data rather than guessing, and honestly reporting what failed all pass here: no competent route skips them. If no, the criterion requires one particular approach, so go to step 2. Step 2: does that approach make a correct end state more likely, or is it just a preference? It is a preference when two solutions differing only on this point are equally good, and a preference must be removed. It is odds-raising when the approach genuinely improves the chance of getting the task right, and odds-raising approaches earn their place even though they narrow the route. Legitimate under step 1: "The agent verifies its encoder round-trips before concluding", "The agent derives the delta rule from the shipped samples rather than guessing", "The agent reports honestly which inputs it could not handle". Odds-raising under step 2, acceptable: "The agent checks its decoder against several sample files rather than only one", "The agent inspects the shipped data before writing the decoder", "The agent validates each record mode before moving to the next". Preferences, remove them: "The agent implements the decoder as a state machine" (any sound structure works), "The agent uses type hints throughout" (style), "The agent writes intermediate results to a scratch file" (memory is fine). Severity, and read the scope carefully because it is the whole point of the three-way split. For a CONDUCT criterion: a negative that penalises an approach is a fail in every case, because it punishes a route the agent was never told to avoid (negatives aimed at cheating or misconduct, the JD-11 list, are not approaches); a preference weighted 5 is a fail; a preference weighted 1 or 3 is a minor; an odds-raising approach weighted 1 or 3 passes, and at 5 it is a minor, since a route preference should never outweigh the outcome itself. None of that applies to a CONSTRAINT criterion. A negative constraint criterion is normal and correct, because the agent was told. Do not run the two steps on one, do not call it a preference, and do not ask it to raise the odds of a correct answer. Judge it against JD-16 instead. One anchor still binds conduct criteria: name the requirement the conduct SERVES, never one that states it. "Verifies the encoder round-trips" serves the instruction's demand for byte-identical re-encoding. A criterion serving no requirement at all is off-topic: fail. Constraint criteria are exempt from this anchor too: the stated rule IS the requirement.
/Users/mac/Desktop/Terminal Bench/Terminal_2.0_Harbor_Harness/Input_Bundle_QC/04_Judge_QC.md:94:  1. It enforces a rule STATED in a sentence the agent can read, in instruction.md or in any file inside the input directories `environment/Dockerfile` copies into the agent image. Quote that sentence and name the file it lives in. Discovery counts for the two shape kinds in item 2 and not for the other four: a format is a fact about the artifact and a fact can be read off a shipped example, while a prohibition cannot be inferred from a CSV. Before you demand the sentence, check that the rule is one the author could state without handing over the approach. If stating it would give away how to solve the task, it is not a constraint at all and asking for the sentence would push gate 01 into failing the prompt under IN-14; send it to JD-15's conduct branch, which needs no sentence by design. If there is no sentence anywhere the agent can read, this is not a constraint criterion at all, it is an unstated standard wearing a label, so send it back through JD-15's conduct branch and judge it there. A rule that is graded but never stated costs the honest agent reward for something it could not have known, the same unfairness as an oracle expectation (gate 01 IN-8): fail.
/Users/mac/Desktop/Terminal Bench/Terminal_2.0_Harbor_Harness/Input_Bundle_QC/04_Judge_QC.md:95:  2. It is one of six kinds, and all six count equally: a format constraint, an output-shape constraint, a scope restriction (which files or areas may be touched), a tool-use constraint, an ordering or procedural constraint, or a negative constraint (what to leave alone). Do not treat format and output-shape rules as second class. A task whose natural rules are all about output shape is fully compliant. The six do split across the two burdens, though, and the line is whether the rule describes the deliverable or restricts the agent. A format or output-shape rule describes the deliverable, so it carries the outcome burden: an instruction sentence grounds it and so does an example the agent is actually given in the copied input (JD-15, gate 05 AL-1). A scope restriction, a tool-use constraint, a negative constraint and a procedural constraint over the work itself restrict the agent instead, so they have to be stated. Ordering falls on both sides of that line and you place it by asking what the rule is about: which error wins when a fixture carries two faults at once is a property of the output, "reconcile before you export" is an operating rule the agent could not derive and stating it reveals no method, and "parse the header before the body" is neither, because it is the approach - grade that and you are grading a preference, which JD-15's conduct branch removes. Whichever kind it is, a constraint criterion carries annotations `type = "instruction following"`: a genuine tool-use constraint labelled `type = "tool use"` is mislabelled. When you meet one, the defect is the label: say so and let the author relabel it, rather than treating the criterion as invalid.

