ssify before you judge. An OUTCOME criterion, which asserts something about what was produced, is a specification item and carries the same burden as a test: point to the instruction sentence, or to the determinate fact in the copied input, or fail it. A CONSTRAINT criterion enforces a rule of engagement the contract states out loud: how the output must be shaped, which files may be touched, which tool must be used, what order the work happens in, what is left alone. A rule of engagement is not the same kind of thing as a fact about the data, and the loose grounding above does not reach it: it must be STATED, in a sentence the agent can read, in the instruction or in any file inside the input directories `environment/Dockerfile` copies into the agent image. No amount of discovery substitutes, because a prohibition cannot be inferred from a CSV. Two exceptions sit on either side of that. A rule about the shape of the output keeps the test burden above, a shipped example included, because a shape is a fact about the artifact rather than a restriction on the agent. And a rule nobody could state without giving away the approach is not a constraint either: demanding its sentence would force the method into the instruction, which gate 01 fails under IN-14, so judge it as conduct. Quote the sentence and name its file. It is graded because it was stated, so compliance is the requirement in itself and you must not ask it to improve the odds of a correct answer. A negative constraint criterion is normal and correct here. What fails is a constraint criterion with no sentence to quote anywhere the agent can read, because that is an unstated rule costing the honest agent reward (gate 01 IN-15 finds the same defect from the instruction side). A CONDUCT criterion, which judges how the agent worked (derivation method, verification habits, honest reporting, restraint, safety), has no instruction sentence behind it and needs none, because demanding one would force the method into the